# Milestone: v0.8.2 (STM32F4 Mandelbrot demo)
# Covers: polled-flag abort / restart pattern used by the Mandelbrot demo
#         (examples/stm32f4/mandelbrot/mandelbrot.u render_level + render_all).
#         A long-running compute loop polls a Realm-global flag once per
#         iteration; an `at` watcher flips the flag mid-loop in response to
#         a side-effect of the compute itself; the loop terminates early
#         on the next poll; an outer "restart" cycle resets the flag and
#         re-enters the same compute, demonstrating the restart half of
#         the pattern works too.
#
# Why a fixture: the production version of this pattern depends on the
# scheduler delivering a safepoint between each iteration of the compute's
# `while` loop AND on the watcher dispatch picking up the dirty cond
# during that safepoint.  Both halves are exercised by individual
# unit tests but the end-to-end "compute body writes -> watcher sees ->
# flag visible on next iter" interaction is what would regress most
# silently if the loader-strand or watcher-eval paths changed.  The
# Mandelbrot demo flips the flag from an ISR (button) or a host-timer
# (gyro) -- both inject events that fire the watcher between strand
# steps -- and the v1.0 .chk runner has no clean equivalent.  Driving
# the cond off the compute's own writes is the closest pure-urbi
# analogue and exercises the same dispatch path.
#
# v0.13.1 (refactor-3 FE-01/B3): counter expectation moved 4 -> 5.  The
# pre-fix back-edge JMP landed one instruction short of loop_start, on
# the YIELD emitted for the `;` before the while -- re-executing it every
# iteration gave the watcher body an extra (unspecified) scheduling point
# per iteration.  With the back-edge corrected, each iteration has exactly
# the one yield its body's `;` specifies: the cond-eval and the body run
# on consecutive yields, so the flag flip is visible one poll later.

var counter = 0
var abort_flag = false
at (Realm.counter > 3) Realm.abort_flag = true
var compute = function() { var i = 0; while (i < 10) { if (Realm.abort_flag == false) { Realm.counter = Realm.counter + 1 } else { i = 10 }; i = i + 1 } }
compute()
Realm.counter
Realm.abort_flag
Realm.counter = 0
Realm.abort_flag = false
compute()
Realm.counter
[00000000] 0
[00000000] false
[00000000] nil
[00000000] <?>
[00000000] nil
[00000000] 5
[00000000] true
[00000000] 0
[00000000] false
[00000000] nil
[00000000] 5
